WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.559 --> 00:00:05.100
MAX COPPIN: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.600
Well, thank you for
joining me here.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.590
Well done for finding your
way over from [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.260
You've passed the first test.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.860
I'm sure it feels like it's
been a long day already,

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.810
particularly for those of
you like me who've come from

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.560
slightly further east,
as you can probably

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.510
tell by the accent.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.600
So my name is Max Coppin.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.820
I'm based in our London office.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.500
I work where our Android
Enterprise efforts are based.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.640
Just to give you a
bit of background,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.070
I've had, counting it up,
about 15 years of experience

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.550
in various different mobile
technology companies,

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:41.550
various different roles.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.230
So developer through to
admin, various different sort

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.710
of experiences of
different technologies,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.830
back from the days when
mobile email was exciting.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.340
Push email was yet
to be a thing, which

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:57.660
now seems like a very,
very long time ago

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.170
and makes me feel
very old indeed.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:06.410
But I'm going to take you
through the strategies

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.850
around Android and
Chrome, how you

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.950
can use some of
these technologies

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:15.200
hopefully to improve your
enterprise experience.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.950
Just a quick look
at the agenda--

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.720
so we're going to look at some
research and some analysis

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.710
from some of the leading
companies in this space.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.180
A bit around
strategy development,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.760
selecting and
deploying the tools.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.270
Some examples of where
some of our partners

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.750
have really embraced this.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:40.280
And then last, but by no means
least, some of the resources

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.280
that we have to help you
go and replicate this

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.630
in your organization.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:52.490
So we've got some research
from our friends at CCS Insight

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.280
that I'm sure some of you
are familiar with that we're

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.170
going to use here.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.230
So really, we're
looking at the maturity

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.030
levels of organizations.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:09.350
And this is real spread from
some very kind of basic,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:13.070
if you like, sort of
elementary levels of deployment

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.290
right through to, as
hopefully you can see,

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.110
some of the more
advanced technologies

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.440
and really kind of
transformational type solutions

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.810
that companies are
looking to put out there.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.890
So what we see really
is that there's

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.420
a big step change
between these-- well,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.770
it can be a big step change
between these different steps,

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.570
these different stages.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.270
And there are a few
points that we'll

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.190
call out that really aid that.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.670
So this center of excellence
you can see in the middle

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:46.430
is something that often
gets pointed at as, say,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.190
a way in which you can really
help move organizations

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.100
through these different stages.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.620
And we'll come on to numbers
that back that up in a minute,

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:01.520
but really the idea of giving
employees a place to go to get

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.660
help in terms of deploying a
mobile solution and that kind

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.540
of thing.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.450
The other area that
we hear of as kind

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.470
of particularly key and
very important going forward

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.540
is the idea of
when you're getting

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.167
to high levels of maturity of
really measuring that success

00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:18.500
and not just measuring adoption.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.190
You know, we deployed
application x to whatever

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:26.120
percentage of employees, but
the real kind of business impact

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.750
of that, how that actually
changed the business,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.660
saved time, that kind of thing.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.920
And the other interesting
insight that we see quite a lot

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:45.950
is there's a pretty clear
alignment between a company

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:50.390
success and their
actual maturity level

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:51.980
here so that there
really is kind

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.130
of a lot of proof
around business

00:03:55.130 --> 00:04:00.050
success and mobility maturity.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.080
So if we take a look at some
more research around these kind

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.450
of apps that typically
used, the first column

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.630
shows really these more common
sort of collaboration apps.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.760
I think there's a bit
of a question mark,

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.010
really, as to whether
these are going

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:20.870
to be the sort of
IT-deployed, corporately

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:24.290
managed applications, or whether
they're more sort of guerrilla

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.220
IT or whatever you
want to call it,

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.520
but consumerization
of IT type deployments

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.160
where employees are
using the apps maybe

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.390
in a kind of an
unsanctioned way.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:40.250
But then as we move
through, we get

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:41.870
to these kind of occasional--

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:45.800
typically it seems to be sort
of approvals or like emergency

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.310
access type applications.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.520
And really, it's only the kind
of single-digit percentages

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.420
in this rare section, which
is where hopefully everyone's

00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:01.400
trying to get to, but
it's the particularly

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.300
small number of applications
being adopted there.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.690
So those are the really kind
of line-of-business, more

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:13.180
transformational applications
that have been deployed.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.960
But there's another
kind of question

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.900
that we'll come onto a
bit later, which is really

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:22.700
around this
relationship between IT

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.960
and the employee in terms who's
driving the adoption, who's

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.200
making the decisions, who holds
the budget, all the things that

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:37.340
are, I guess, points of friction
and potentially contention.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.170
So a few other numbers to
run through around this.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.100
So average number of apps
used by employees for work

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.810
is apparently 4.1.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.080
I don't know what that 0.1
overlap is, but there you go.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.210
But apparently 86%
of employees say

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:56.960
that they do use or do regularly
use applications at work.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.210
But it's just that 4.1
is the average number

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.450
of different apps
that they're using.

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.980
Now, that number's obviously
something that we predict

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.560
or certainly Gartner predicts to
increase hugely over the years.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.960
There is one stat
that I've heard

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.750
or one prediction I've heard
that sounds rather scary

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:18.620
is that they talked about the
idea of the average company

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:24.530
producing 1,400 new apps
by 2024, which sounds

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:28.520
like a kind of extraordinary
number, a kind of scary number,

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.530
a bit of an extreme number
[INAUDIBLE] whatever

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.410
it is, around 200 a year.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.920
And it will be five
times more than IT can

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:40.050
cope with in terms of demand.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.450
So great news.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.030
Isn't that just what you
wanted to hear on a Wednesday

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.950
afternoon?

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:49.430
But it's an extraordinary
number, given that in--

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.110
I think it's 2016,
[INAUDIBLE] did

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:56.120
a survey where they said
80% of the organizations

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.615
that they work with only have
less than 10 applications

00:06:59.615 --> 00:07:01.130
deployed.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:03.800
So there's this
huge curve coming,

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.340
and a whole load of
challenges and complexities

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.200
associated with that.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.437
Quite where all these
applications are

00:07:12.437 --> 00:07:15.020
going to get written and who's
going to do all that developing

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.190
is another good question.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.370
Although, if anyone has a kid--

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:19.740
anyone got kids under 3?

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.200
Stick your hand in the air.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:21.905
Just me?

00:07:21.905 --> 00:07:23.060
There we go, a few there.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.970
Have you ever heard of the
Fisher Price Coda-a-pillar?

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:26.570
Be scared.

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:27.380
Be very scared.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:31.130
It's a coding toy, and to
teach kids from three years

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:35.180
on to code, so very scary.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:38.490
I'm sure my daughter will
be looking forward to that.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:43.760
So a huge uptake in
applications, and a huge uptake

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.967
in the increase in the speed
at which these applications are

00:07:46.967 --> 00:07:49.550
going to be developed, which is
another kind of challenge when

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.610
you look at the
typical enterprise

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:58.100
deployment or development cycles
are sort of 18 to 24 months.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.590
Whereas now, you're
seeing requirements

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:04.400
where you're looking
at weeks and even days

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.170
in terms of deployment cycles.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.760
So again, Gartner's
prediction is

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.220
to look at this
bimodal approach where

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:14.750
you say we're going to have some
applications that are typically

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.449
more like legacy,
longstanding applications

00:08:17.449 --> 00:08:19.490
that we're going to develop
with one methodology.

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.073
And then we're going to go more
agile for something that maybe

00:08:22.073 --> 00:08:24.890
is tied to a specific
event, or has

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:26.870
a finite lifetime
and a completely

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:28.586
different set of requirements.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:35.320
So 41% of employees
say mobile has already

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.470
changed the way they work.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:41.220
That kind of makes me think
it's possibly a little bit low,

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.440
but again, those
previous numbers

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.410
suggest that it's going to
change pretty drastically

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:50.840
over the coming years.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:54.220
And this is something that maybe
backs up that previous number

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.200
and where the change
is going to come from.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.430
So 76% of employees have not
asked their IT department

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:01.990
to provide apps for work.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:04.790
So why do you think that is?

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.540
75% of them said they
would not ask IT,

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:15.330
because IT would say no or
ignore their request, so quite

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:19.360
a simple answer, but
quite a challenging one

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.380
when you think if IT are kind
of seen as blocking, then

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:27.100
what typically happens is we'll
see employees go around IT

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.090
and find their own
way of doing things.

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:33.000
It's the scenario of, I want to
be able to annotate that PDF.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.960
You're not going to give
me the tools to do it,

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.950
so I'll forward it
to my personal email.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:38.950
And then I'll go and do
it on my personal device

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:40.470
or whatever it is.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:44.470
And suddenly you're out of
that kind of sphere of control.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:50.410
So there's good reasons for
trying to help employees

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:53.470
through these challenges.

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.720
And so the lack of
a formal process

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.300
for requesting
applications really

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:02.350
seems to be the big challenge.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:05.980
Really at least having a
way of allowing employees

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:09.190
to request apps helps
shape your demand

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.340
in terms of types
of applications,

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.190
in terms of volumes,
and helps you really

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:19.090
segment users and all
these best practices

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:23.560
around determining what it
is that you need to start

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:27.010
providing to your employees.

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:29.902
Anything where you're getting
numbers to back up decisions

00:10:29.902 --> 00:10:31.360
is always going to
be a good thing.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:37.790
So 72% of employees do not
have a company app store.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:43.800
So I think here there's
a lot of user behavior

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.380
and so on from
the consumer world

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:50.190
where you have an application
to do just about anything.

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:51.390
And you go to your store.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.600
You search for whatever it
is, whatever hotel you're in

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.720
or airline or city.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.550
And you type in whatever
it is, and you'll

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:01.680
find an application for it.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.960
And that's typically how
users find applications.

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:09.190
If you don't have that set
up within your organization,

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:12.660
then how are users going
to find the application?

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:16.050
And I think what we've
certainly seen from the Google

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.750
side historically is that the
first thing that an enterprise

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:22.830
would try and do would be to
block access to Google Play

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:24.422
and distribute
apps their own way.

00:11:24.422 --> 00:11:26.130
And I'll come on to
it a little bit later

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:30.630
about how we can negate the need
to do that and deliver a much

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:33.331
better application experience.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:39.030
So when we look at this
sort of market and the types

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.107
of applications out
there, as we've seen,

00:11:42.107 --> 00:11:44.190
the bulk of applications,
they're really just kind

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.560
of packaged,
off-the-shelf, SaaS--

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:50.820
I'm extending some
existing application--

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:55.140
It may be collaboration
or that kind of thing.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:12:00.360
Moving up through those steps
is this existing maybe approval

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.140
mobilization, desktop
process, et cetera, et cetera.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:07.490
The top is where we
really aspire to get to

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:10.500
and where I think the
most benefit can be had.

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.380
And these are
typically applications

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:16.560
that are maybe mobile-first
or even mobile-only

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.900
where you're really
taking advantage

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:23.590
of the power of the
device to deliver

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.460
a very different,
unique, and again,

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:28.590
transformational experience.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:36.700
So with that in mind, there's
one bit of information

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:39.130
that never seems
to change around

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:43.870
importance in terms of
spend, and security is always

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:45.730
up there.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:49.220
Mobility always seems to go
hand-in-hand with security.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:52.230
It opens up arguably
a number of other--

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:55.650
or certainly used to open up a
number of other attack vectors,

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.680
and this kind of thing.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:12:58.519
And I think--

00:12:58.519 --> 00:13:00.310
I'd like to think that
things have moved on

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:03.490
slightly from where we were a
few years ago, in that security

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:06.860
used to be very much
a blocker in mobility

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:10.870
and that we could
not deploy devices,

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:12.700
applications,
whatever, because there

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.570
is no secure way of doing it.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.110
I'd like to think we've
kind of got beyond that now

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:18.820
and that there is
a way of doing it.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:22.360
There may be sort of
regulatory or other kind

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:26.616
of reasons around that, that
adds the complexity to it.

00:13:26.616 --> 00:13:28.240
But I think really
that we don't really

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.580
see, based on
conversations that we

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:33.340
have with customers,
security actually

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:35.110
stopping the deployment.

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:40.030
I think a lot of the reason that
security is a priority going

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:42.620
forward is around some of
the new legislation coming

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:47.560
in-- so things like GDPR, which
hopefully you're familiar with.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:49.800
If not, go and google it--

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.000
a plug for our search friends.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.157
But go and have a look at that.

00:13:54.157 --> 00:13:55.990
And that has some pretty
strict requirements

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.086
around reporting
of data breaches

00:13:59.086 --> 00:14:00.460
and this kind of
thing, which are

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:05.871
going to drive some changes
in security and mobility usage

00:14:05.871 --> 00:14:06.370
patterns.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.770
So there's a number of
different analysis firms

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:19.370
and reports have
talked about how

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:21.370
Android's going to rise
in the enterprise world.

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:24.520
It's going to really
change things going forward

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:29.440
in terms of not just replacing
typical knowledge worker,

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:34.572
BlackBerry deployments,
but also in the kind of--

00:14:34.572 --> 00:14:37.450
these are all
different use cases

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.412
and line-of-business,
purpose-built, rugged, et

00:14:40.412 --> 00:14:41.900
cetera type deployments.

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.620
Some of you might have seen
my colleague presenting

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.750
on this kind of thing earlier.

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:51.790
But that's where there's a huge,
I guess, area of opportunity

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:53.890
to tick off a lot of
those boxes in terms

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:59.800
of big ticket, really
important enterprise

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:02.380
changing application
deployments.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.570
So not just, what was
I doing at the desk.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.430
Now I don't need to
do it at the desk--

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:10.510
really going beyond that.

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:14.830
And there's a whole slew
of feature requirements,

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:18.040
these newer technologies, that
I guess are not immediately

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.620
things that are thought of
as enterprise technologies,

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:23.920
but things like
voice and assistance

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:29.620
and so on where if you're
looking at a device that

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:31.554
is going to be operated
by someone with gloves

00:15:31.554 --> 00:15:33.220
in extreme conditions
and so on, they're

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:35.590
not going to be able
to necessarily use

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:39.210
even a keyboard, let
alone a touch screen.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.660
And so embracing technologies
like voice control and so on

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:46.320
can really help that.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:50.782
And there's a huge
range of Android devices

00:15:50.782 --> 00:15:52.990
out there, which I'm sure
for a lot of you would have

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:54.670
historically seen as
a bit of a challenge

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:55.628
and a bit of a problem.

00:15:55.628 --> 00:15:58.720
And the F word always
used to come up--

00:15:58.720 --> 00:15:59.530
"fragmentation."

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:02.920
Nothing more extreme
than that, before I get

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:04.100
bleeped on the video.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.610
But that's something
that, again, we'll

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:08.470
talk about in a minute
how we've hopefully

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.745
solved and kind of flattened
out that landscape for you,

00:16:11.745 --> 00:16:13.930
and made it really
easy to deploy

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:17.170
a range of different
[INAUDIBLE] devices

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:20.680
across the organization for
completely different use cases.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:27.080
So in terms of strategy
development, there's a--

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:29.520
I could go on forever,
really, with a list

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:32.540
of things to consider here.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:34.847
What is the aim, and
why are you doing this,

00:16:34.847 --> 00:16:36.430
and who are we doing
it for are things

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:39.050
that may sound straightforward
and silly questions,

00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:41.150
but I think that
often get ignored,

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:45.140
because it's easy to skip
too far down the line

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.860
and go, right, what's
our IT strategy?

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:50.110
Well, we already kind of
know where we want to get to.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:52.824
And let's just work
back from there.

00:16:52.824 --> 00:16:53.990
And where we want to get to.

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.330
Maybe something a bit
more basic around maybe

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:58.240
deploying MDM or whatever.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.320
But really by going
through these questions

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:04.089
and looking at what
we're trying to do

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:06.980
I think is a really
key way of doing it.

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:09.500
The other interesting
thing we always hear

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:13.819
is around what stage
companies think they're at.

00:17:13.819 --> 00:17:18.250
So hands up here who would say
they've got an IT strategy.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:20.750
Anyone confident enough
to say that they've one,

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:22.130
or even have written down one?

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:24.680
I mean, that's the other
thing that we really don't see

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:31.871
much of is people documenting
and iterating on that document.

00:17:31.871 --> 00:17:34.370
Period of time it needs to cover
is another interesting one.

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:39.110
I mean, typically, businesses
may have a one-year strategy,

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.840
and an IT strategy's going to
be over three to five years.

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:43.285
Aligning business
and IT strategy

00:17:43.285 --> 00:17:46.340
are pretty key, but not
necessarily achieved,

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:49.230
particularly with staff
turnover and so on--

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.794
another good reason for
documenting these things.

00:17:51.794 --> 00:17:53.960
And measuring results and
iterating-- so like I say,

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:56.000
measuring the success
and the change

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.850
that you're having,
not just the adoption.

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.710
So my favorite
examples of what's

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:10.600
not a strategy, which are
all scenarios that I've

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.510
had personal experience of--

00:18:13.510 --> 00:18:16.450
the days of
BlackBerry panic where

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.990
everyone had to throw
a BlackBerry into all

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:22.720
of their senior employees'
hands because the law firm

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.760
next door was doing it.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:26.060
It's not really a strategy.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:29.980
It's a sort of
reactionary tactic

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:34.960
to try and often get
a bit of appeasement.

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:38.764
"I need to get the CEO's iPad
working" was another famous--

00:18:38.764 --> 00:18:40.180
I think it was one
Christmas where

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:42.370
it seemed like every
CEO, CIO, et cetera,

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:45.407
went home, got an
iPad, threw it at IT,

00:18:45.407 --> 00:18:47.740
who then went, oh god, what
are we going to do with this

00:18:47.740 --> 00:18:50.090
and how are we going
to get this working?

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:53.320
And that generally moved
into the bottom state

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:55.110
of, I need to be
able to lock, locate,

00:18:55.110 --> 00:19:00.130
wipe all these devices,
and then that challenge

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:05.650
of personal devices moving
into enterprise world,

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:08.930
the whole scenario
of whole-device wipe.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:12.680
And that lead into containers
and that kind of thing.

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:16.000
I know I genuinely have had a
conversation with a company who

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:18.340
were that kind of one
company who seemed to be

00:19:18.340 --> 00:19:20.410
the one who were being taken--

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:22.960
I think actually taken
to court by someone

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:25.334
because they wiped
their device, and it had

00:19:25.334 --> 00:19:26.500
pictures of their cat on it.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:28.820
So it really does happen.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:30.310
It's not just that
kind of example

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:32.060
that people who are trying to
sell you a container solution

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:32.560
talk about.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:38.750
So a few other
considerations on this slide.

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:41.430
So engage the line of business.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.070
So I'd say think outside
of the IT department,

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:47.980
and get out there and work
with these line of business.

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:49.510
There's one company
that we've been

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:51.310
doing some work with where--

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:52.810
an airline who were
looking at what

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:57.370
they can do around working
with their employees.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:01.180
And they've gone to the extent
of a number of their IT staff

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:04.150
have trained as cabin crew so
they can go out and experience

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:06.670
what the cabin crew
experience, and better

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:08.046
understand the
kind of challenges

00:20:08.046 --> 00:20:09.170
that they're going through.

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:10.970
So that might be quite
an extreme example,

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:15.760
but it's a very good example
of living the business problem

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:20.840
rather than working it through
in the confines of the IT

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:22.240
department.

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:26.450
And often working
with business--

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:28.840
this phrase of
"citizen developers,"

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:30.580
which I'm sure you've heard of--

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:34.700
all these kids who grew
up with their Fisher Price

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.910
Coda-a-pillar and
move into companies

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:43.690
and start writing applications
to solve business problems.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:45.790
Going back to those numbers
that we saw earlier,

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:50.440
it's really that
scenario of, if there

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:54.050
isn't an application supplied to
me to solve that problem, I'll

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:57.380
go and write my own, if I don't
have a good way of requesting

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:59.860
that from IT and so on.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:03.220
So if someone's motivated
enough to write an application

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.230
and solve the
problem themselves,

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:10.870
then that's someone that you
really should be working with.

00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:12.970
Survey data are,
again, kind of key--

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:15.910
so not just the feedback form,
how did you find this device,

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:17.890
was this application
useful, but you

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:20.440
can go a level deeper and
look at the actual analytics

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:21.500
within applications.

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:22.570
What are people doing?

00:21:22.570 --> 00:21:24.028
If you're deploying
an application,

00:21:24.028 --> 00:21:26.534
what are they doing
within the application?

00:21:26.534 --> 00:21:28.450
Which screens are they
spending their time on?

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:32.020
What are the actual actions
that they're going through?

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:35.360
It's very easy using a number
of our tools and others

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:39.430
to collect that data and
do the analytics there.

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:41.920
And avoiding these what
are called top-drawer

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:43.930
deployments where you go, great.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:44.439
Thank you.

00:21:44.439 --> 00:21:46.480
Thank you, IT, for giving
me this wonderful tool.

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.430
I'm going to stick
it in the top drawer

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:51.340
because you've turned
this wonderful smartphone

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:54.197
into essentially the
internet-connected paper weight

00:21:54.197 --> 00:21:55.280
and it's now no use to me.

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:01.990
So again, some
basic-sounding things,

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:05.372
but often somewhat
overlooked-- what

00:22:05.372 --> 00:22:06.580
are the outcomes going to be?

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:09.100
So we should have a
tool that is really

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:13.270
going to help our
employees do a better job.

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:16.480
It does raise a whole
other set of questions

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:19.510
around who chooses the
tool, who's responsible,

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:21.250
who pays for it.

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:23.290
And often, these
are decisions that

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:27.220
are more led by regional
variations, legislation,

00:22:27.220 --> 00:22:28.720
and so on.

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.220
But there's a whole
bunch of reasons

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:33.100
that we'll come
into, how you could

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:35.509
work through these challenges.

00:22:35.509 --> 00:22:40.800
So moving on on tool selection.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:47.690
So we've got a whole bunch
of Google devices running

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:51.120
a couple of different OSs, or
a few different OSs out there

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:53.640
that are essentially,
as one of my colleagues

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.000
always calls them--

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:57.410
or at least he did until the
[INAUDIBLE] came around--

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:59.190
glowing rectangles.

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.140
So you choose your
glowing rectangle.

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:04.710
You leverage the power
of the cloud, which

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:06.940
you've heard so much
about this morning,

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:08.910
and our various
different services there

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:14.502
in order to deploy
whatever application

00:23:14.502 --> 00:23:16.710
it is that you're trying to
deploy and solve whatever

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:18.001
problem you're trying to solve.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.970
So if we drill down into what
that means in terms of Android

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:31.440
and Chrome world, we've got
a whole range of devices

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:36.420
to cover, every price
point, use case,

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:40.380
from these convertible tablets
to rugged devices that you

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:42.420
can drive over and so on.

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:48.000
So if we look at what that
means in the Android world,

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.900
I mentioned earlier
the F word and what

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:55.140
we've done to try
and improve that,

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:58.950
that challenge that we gave
to you guys historically.

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:00.570
And I think that
the main message now

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:04.110
is that if you go out and
acquire an Android device,

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:06.810
as long as it has GMS, the
Google Mobile Services,

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:11.430
on there, then it will have all
of the enterprise capabilities

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.610
that you'll need baked
into the platform.

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:17.130
So that's the work
that we've been doing--

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:20.580
fully integrating
this into the platform

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:23.490
and enabling a number of
our ecosystem partners

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:27.420
to really leverage
that technology.

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:33.960
So starting with Lollipop,
we introduced this

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:40.070
into the framework around the
support for the device owner

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:43.950
and a separate managed
work profile on the device.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:46.350
And that was really
the core of what was

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:48.870
introduced into the framework.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:54.840
With Marshmallow, we introduced
some APIs, what we call COSU.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.440
So it's Corporate-Owned,
Singe-Use deployment-- so

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.470
really, a kind of rugged,
line-of-business kiosk device.

00:25:01.470 --> 00:25:02.640
Call it what you will.

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:05.340
Choose your own
acronym, but a device

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:07.980
that is going to be really
aimed for a single purpose

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:11.340
and only running one
or two applications.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:14.600
And then we step through to
[? N. ?] And obviously, we'll

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.700
continue on to O, just adding
really incremental features

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:20.970
and improving a lot
around the user experience

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:24.980
and those sort of things.

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:29.290
Now along side that, we've done
a lot of work on Google Play.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:32.500
So I mentioned earlier
how the first thing

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:35.410
that a lot of IT
departments would do

00:25:35.410 --> 00:25:39.850
would be to block Play, take
it off devices somehow, disable

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:42.080
it, and deploy
applications another way.

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:46.520
Well, some of you may have
caught Adrian's talk earlier,

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:50.650
but Play is actually
a very powerful tool

00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:54.720
for enterprise security now.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:57.550
So one of the key things that
we're trying to avoid companies

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:01.920
having to do is enable unknown
sources on their devices,

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:04.130
so this idea of
sidelining applications

00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:06.100
that don't come from Play.

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:08.245
And the reason for
that is because any

00:26:08.245 --> 00:26:09.980
of the applications
that go through Play

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:12.927
go through a huge amount of
scanning that is increasingly

00:26:12.927 --> 00:26:14.760
leveraging our machine
learning capabilities

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:19.450
and so on and so forth to ensure
that these applications are

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:23.180
trusted, secure applications.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:25.390
So we support a number of
other features in terms

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:29.740
of [INAUDIBLE] purchase
and managed configuration--

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:32.800
so this idea of rather
than presenting a user

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:34.600
with an application
which they then

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:39.610
need to enter a user name and
a server address and a port

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:42.070
and a whole load of config
info, which they're either going

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:44.110
to get wrong and
drive support calls,

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:46.720
or not do and therefore
not use the app, we now

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:50.680
have a consistent
way of ISVs allowing

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:53.790
their applications to be
configured across the board

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:56.110
by an EMS.

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:58.690
So all of these
features are essentially

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:02.635
leveraged by whichever EMM
is used, be that G Suite

00:27:02.635 --> 00:27:06.310
or be it one of the many
partners that we have.

00:27:10.174 --> 00:27:12.090
The other thing I should
mention at this point

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:15.600
is that there's no
requirement necessarily

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:18.540
for your application
to be public.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:23.230
So another common bit of
feedback that we got was,

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:25.650
we have an
application that we're

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:28.380
going to deploy internally
to our however many thousand

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.050
devices, and that's the
only application we want,

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:33.570
or these 10 applications
are the only ones we want.

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:37.950
Why would we look at
using something like Play?

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:41.550
And so we've given
you a way of providing

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.190
not just managed
Google Play and saying,

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:47.280
I want to provide a subset
of the public applications

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:51.015
in Play, but you're
also able to publish,

00:27:51.015 --> 00:27:54.697
through a private channel,
your own internal applications,

00:27:54.697 --> 00:27:57.030
again, through the same kind
of similar user experience.

00:27:57.030 --> 00:28:00.460
So from a user perspective,
I'm going into my Play store

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:06.050
and I'm searching for an
application, and it's there.

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:09.990
It's just that you as IT know
that it's already been approved

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:11.605
and deployed accordingly.

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:18.820
And what these two
Android platform features

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:22.650
and the managed Google Play
are really trying to help you

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:25.870
guys do is move
through the steps

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:28.465
and through the maturity
curves that we looked

00:28:28.465 --> 00:28:34.180
at earlier from the kind of
traditional knowledge worker

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:38.320
email and collaboration tool
really down to this far end

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:46.450
where devices on forklift trucks
and really rugged, very much

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:49.450
"out of the office" scenarios.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:53.050
So we have a solution
for the knowledge worker.

00:28:53.050 --> 00:28:56.560
We actually have this
device owner concept

00:28:56.560 --> 00:28:59.020
where you can manage the whole
device in a very much kind

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:01.000
of traditional MDM.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:03.860
We have this [INAUDIBLE]
for millennials

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:10.180
with wacky haircuts where you
can have a separate managed

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.560
profile on a personal device.

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:14.570
And again, all of
the usual boxes

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:17.230
ticked in terms of IT
just having visibility

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:20.530
into this managed profile and
nothing beyond, and no one

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:23.760
having their cat pictures wiped.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:26.020
The single-use-- like I
said, COSU, as we call it--

00:29:26.020 --> 00:29:30.520
API set where I'm really
locking a device down

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:35.310
into a specific application
or set of applications.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:38.770
And then really
beyond that, it's

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:42.120
limited only by the imagination
of yourselves and the OEMs

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:46.918
to produce a device that
will meet those requirements.

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:52.390
So I mentioned the OEMs there.

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:55.570
These are some of our key
partners on the far left.

00:29:55.570 --> 00:30:00.610
And this is a subset of our
ever-growing list of partners.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:04.810
But this gives you an idea
of the kind of ecosystem

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:05.840
we're operating with.

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:08.170
So device manufacturers
are obviously key.

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:12.010
We need them to be shipping
Androids and [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:14.680
versions of Android.

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:16.460
Much like the carriers
and the approvals

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:21.740
that we go through, those
guys, AMM, MDM, MAM--

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:24.850
call it what you will--
partners are also

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:26.800
really leveraging all
of these features that

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:27.810
are baked into Android.

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:31.120
So that's the key
takeaway for those guys

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:35.320
is the features are
in the platform,

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:38.910
and it's the [? MAM ?]
who leverages them.

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:42.040
And then obviously, ISVs are
critical to the deployment

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:46.520
through Play and VPN in a
similar sort of fashion,

00:30:46.520 --> 00:30:48.400
and an SI to pull
it all together.

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:56.930
So if we move onto
the Chrome side--

00:30:56.930 --> 00:30:59.450
and hopefully, you're
familiar at least

00:30:59.450 --> 00:31:02.400
in passing with Chromebooks.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:05.610
And what changed relatively,
or in the last few years,

00:31:05.610 --> 00:31:11.550
was this move from really a
kind of low-price point device

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:15.000
into a few really
enterprise-grade, higher-end

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.268
models.

00:31:17.268 --> 00:31:18.400
And there's a few here.

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:22.710
The ThinkPad, the Dell
device, and the HP device

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:25.470
all tick that box.

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:27.330
But really, again,
there's a story

00:31:27.330 --> 00:31:32.340
beyond the desktop for
Chrome in these kind

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:35.300
of-- going from knowledge
workers on the far left

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:38.820
to signage, particularly as we
get into more exciting things

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:43.620
where we're getting into digital
signage that is dynamically

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:49.170
changing based on a whole
load of cloud-based analytics

00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:50.670
or whatever it may be.

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:55.470
Kiosks, again-- another
route rather than the Android

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:58.299
where it can be Chrome kiosks.

00:31:58.299 --> 00:31:59.840
And Chromebook's
[INAUDIBLE] as well.

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:03.840
So it's something that
we use hugely internally

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:07.150
is Chrome as a
videoconferencing platform.

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:10.950
So again, similar
to Android in that

00:32:10.950 --> 00:32:15.270
you can deploy in a range
of different use cases.

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:17.940
And Chrome's strength
is really, like I say,

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:20.520
is speed, security,
and simplicity.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:26.460
We've had huge success in
the education space because

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:29.970
of these features, really.

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.000
It's very, very easy to
get up and running quickly

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:38.020
with a hugely well-managed
set of devices.

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:40.020
And again, you've got
this consistent management

00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:46.010
experience across
different OEM platforms.

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:48.540
So a quick look at
what you can do--

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:50.680
management-wise, there
are user settings,

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:54.730
which, as you may expect, follow
the user on different devices.

00:32:54.730 --> 00:32:57.570
Device settings are for
that specific device

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:01.400
and often they're inherited
from the user settings.

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:03.730
On the public session
settings, this

00:33:03.730 --> 00:33:07.960
is really for if you
want your Chrome kiosk,

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:09.610
that kind of thing.

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:12.510
And then there's the devices
asset-tracking management.

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:14.470
Who's logged into the devices?

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:18.780
Where and when, and
looking at usage and so on.

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:21.630
So on a user side of
things, what we see

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:24.310
most usage around is
apps and extensions,

00:33:24.310 --> 00:33:25.980
customizing their web store.

00:33:25.980 --> 00:33:30.086
Route through a proxy's
very widely used.

00:33:30.086 --> 00:33:33.010
It's sort of a
non-VPN way of getting

00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:35.270
enterprise connectivity,
and right down

00:33:35.270 --> 00:33:40.691
to hardware management, such
as web cams in this slightly

00:33:40.691 --> 00:33:41.690
bizarre-looking example.

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:51.440
Now, to give you more options
or confuse you somewhat,

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:53.870
depending on the
way you look at it,

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:59.000
what we recently launched was
Android apps running on Chrome.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:05.720
So you can take a Chromebook
and have the Google Play

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:07.910
Store on this device.

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:11.610
And it'll run
Android applications.

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:15.020
So one of the
challenges or barriers

00:34:15.020 --> 00:34:18.150
to adoption, if you like,
of Chrome historically

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:21.920
had been that I need
application x, y, z.

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:23.659
It's not available
on the platform,

00:34:23.659 --> 00:34:29.120
and therefore, I can't move to
a full Chromebook environment.

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:30.460
Now, hopefully you can.

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:35.719
You get a full set of Play apps.

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:37.639
It's not a sort of emulator.

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:41.210
There's no need to
customize the application,

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:45.320
and you still get all of the
benefits around application

00:34:45.320 --> 00:34:47.989
management and the
store management

00:34:47.989 --> 00:34:49.219
that we talked about.

00:34:52.600 --> 00:34:56.190
OK, so a couple of
things on deployment.

00:34:56.190 --> 00:34:58.730
So this is one
thing that I think

00:34:58.730 --> 00:35:00.080
often gets kind of overlooked.

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:03.790
And when I said before about
looking at our strategy,

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:06.770
I think often, it's easy
to go into it saying,

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:08.540
let's look IT strategy.

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:10.270
The answer is we
need to deploy an MDM

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:12.440
and lock down all
of these devices.

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:14.750
But let's look at our strategy.

00:35:14.750 --> 00:35:18.360
And I think, yes, there may
be regulatory requirements,

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:21.450
[INAUDIBLE] whatever it may be.

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:24.554
But I think it's kind important
to bear in mind what they are,

00:35:24.554 --> 00:35:25.970
and again, maybe
document and work

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:28.700
through these, because the
most important thing is

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:32.360
we see really is
how that affects

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.150
the user of this technology.

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:37.000
There's a session,
actually, tomorrow--

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:39.894
I think it's in here at 11:20--

00:35:39.894 --> 00:35:41.310
which talks about
how we at Google

00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:44.630
do this internally, which I'd
highly recommend you come to,

00:35:44.630 --> 00:35:48.710
and some of our
approaches around things

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:49.620
like tiered access.

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:54.200
So they'll go into providing
increasing levels of access

00:35:54.200 --> 00:35:56.530
with increasing levels of
security, for example--

00:35:56.530 --> 00:35:57.620
that kind of thing.

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:04.809
Because when it comes
to a user, I think,

00:36:04.809 --> 00:36:06.350
again, it's often
kind of overlooked.

00:36:06.350 --> 00:36:08.360
And we hear a lot
from users of having

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:10.265
to jump through lots
of hurdles, having

00:36:10.265 --> 00:36:13.850
their 16-digit alphanumeric
passcode and so on,

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:15.820
and all they can do
now is get email.

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:18.590
And is this a benefit
to me as a user?

00:36:18.590 --> 00:36:22.010
Am I sure I want
to go through this?

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:26.000
And so it's key
to articulate what

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:28.876
it is that they get from it.

00:36:28.876 --> 00:36:30.500
And so we've seen
some partners come up

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:32.340
with quite innovative
solutions around that.

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:34.465
So I mentioned this kind
of tiered access approach,

00:36:34.465 --> 00:36:38.690
or really letting users have
access to some data and only

00:36:38.690 --> 00:36:42.685
really increasing the level
of enforcement and management

00:36:42.685 --> 00:36:44.060
when they get to
access something

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:45.970
that's deemed more secure.

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:48.730
You don't need to have
two-factor authentication

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:51.320
and these long
passcodes to get email,

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:54.260
but maybe you do if you're
going to be VPNing back

00:36:54.260 --> 00:36:55.860
into the office, for example.

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:00.265
So another few things--

00:37:00.265 --> 00:37:03.300
so the MDM, EMM, and MAM
kind of plays a little bit

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:06.120
into that sort of tiered
access approach of stepping up

00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:10.270
through the security levels.

00:37:10.270 --> 00:37:12.320
Select a partner
or build your own.

00:37:12.320 --> 00:37:14.670
So this is an
increasing trend that we

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:18.780
see in a number of different
areas of companies saying,

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:23.880
well, actually, I'm building
my own applications internally,

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:27.820
and I need light-weight
management or whatever,

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:30.852
and building at least a
portion of that themselves.

00:37:30.852 --> 00:37:32.310
So that's something
that, again, we

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:34.530
have a whole bunch of
tools and resources

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:39.610
to help you go through
that should you want to.

00:37:39.610 --> 00:37:44.250
Migrating EMM and device OS is
another kind of common thread

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:47.130
that we get is around this--

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:51.480
companies moving either
between EMM, having multi-EMM,

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:53.730
it used to be something that
was somewhat challenging.

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:56.010
We've hopefully made
it a lot easier.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:57.350
And also, migrating OS.

00:37:57.350 --> 00:38:02.757
And again, as I mentioned,
there's a lot of rugged devices

00:38:02.757 --> 00:38:05.090
and so on out there that are
moving from maybe a Windows

00:38:05.090 --> 00:38:09.180
CE into the Android world.

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:12.650
And again, there's a bunch
of partners out there

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:15.960
who can help with this
kind of migration story.

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:19.800
User training or
support, again, is

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:22.720
kind of a straightforward,
basic, often forgotten.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:27.030
And yes, there are such things
as other platforms, not just

00:38:27.030 --> 00:38:28.460
Android and Chrome.

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:30.680
And obviously, in a
number of the current G

00:38:30.680 --> 00:38:36.380
Suite in other sessions, you'll
see how we can support those.

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:38.130
So a few examples.

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:41.650
Dan mentioned Home
Depot in her keynote

00:38:41.650 --> 00:38:44.050
this morning, which
hopefully you all caught.

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:46.389
This is actually a
different customer

00:38:46.389 --> 00:38:48.430
that we've been working
with based out of the UK,

00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:52.000
but a multinational
supermarket brand.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.340
And they were doing
exactly this-- kind

00:38:54.340 --> 00:38:57.040
of replacing legacy
Windows devices,

00:38:57.040 --> 00:39:01.780
consolidating down
from the drivers

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:04.770
with a tool belt
full of one device

00:39:04.770 --> 00:39:07.300
to do the barcode scanning, one
device to do the navigation,

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:09.800
one device to do the
signing off and drop off

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:13.270
and that kind of thing,
down into a single device.

00:39:13.270 --> 00:39:18.490
Customer-facing and
employee-facing tablets

00:39:18.490 --> 00:39:20.500
in their warehouses
and in their stores.

00:39:20.500 --> 00:39:25.210
A whole different kind
of slew of use cases.

00:39:25.210 --> 00:39:27.100
And they were dealing
with multi-EMM

00:39:27.100 --> 00:39:29.260
and had this very
much, as I call it,

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:35.110
white-glove deployment for
their head office devices.

00:39:35.110 --> 00:39:39.610
So we they were a great
example of where we can--

00:39:39.610 --> 00:39:43.120
they threw all those tools and
all those funny shaped devices

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:45.610
in one of the earlier
slides, and say,

00:39:45.610 --> 00:39:48.860
well, actually, we can pick
a solution for each of these.

00:39:48.860 --> 00:39:50.860
We've got a different
management mode

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:53.480
for each of these
kind of use cases.

00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:58.150
And by stepping through that, we
can cover all of the use cases

00:39:58.150 --> 00:39:59.982
that they've got.

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:08.640
Another example of where we've
had partnerships success as

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:14.070
well as the customer successes
is Zebra, or "Zeebra"

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:16.200
as I should say in America.

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:20.760
So they're, if you don't know,
a rugged device manufacturer

00:40:20.760 --> 00:40:23.020
with their sort of
symbol heritage,

00:40:23.020 --> 00:40:29.350
so real background in
warehouse and other use cases.

00:40:29.350 --> 00:40:32.890
And when I first started
speaking to these guys,

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:37.650
they were very much against
adopting Google technology

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:42.690
on their platform, and it was
seen as too challenging and too

00:40:42.690 --> 00:40:47.559
much of a really hard sell, if
you like, to their customers.

00:40:47.559 --> 00:40:49.600
But what we were able to
work through, and really

00:40:49.600 --> 00:40:53.740
through these the steps that
we talked about earlier, from L

00:40:53.740 --> 00:40:58.380
to M and into N, was
that because you're

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:04.390
able to take this device which
has the Android and GMS on,

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:08.970
you're able to manage it with
this consistent framework,

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:11.610
you're able to guarantee
security, deploy apps and so

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:14.010
on through Google
Play, it actually

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:16.860
became a real strength
and a benefit to them.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:22.320
And they have really gone
all in on Android and on GMS.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:26.490
So they have professional
services teams doing migration.

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:29.430
They've signed up to
our security patches,

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:31.920
again, which you may
have heard of in some

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:33.420
of the other sessions.

00:41:33.420 --> 00:41:36.180
We release security
patches every 30 days.

00:41:36.180 --> 00:41:38.250
And OEMs take those on board.

00:41:38.250 --> 00:41:41.090
And Zebra have been quick
to adopt that and commit

00:41:41.090 --> 00:41:45.690
to that over a long period,
so for the three years or five

00:41:45.690 --> 00:41:48.390
years that a device
is sold for and three

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:49.590
or five years beyond that.

00:41:49.590 --> 00:41:53.280
So they've really
been a good example

00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:56.300
of where we've gone
from not really

00:41:56.300 --> 00:41:58.470
fitting in that kind of
area in that use case

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:04.830
before through adopting fully
our enterprise feature set.

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:11.520
So I'm just going to run
through a few resources

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:13.530
to help you guys, hopefully.

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:17.430
So one of the more
recent innovations

00:42:17.430 --> 00:42:21.460
that we've delivered is the
Android Management Experience.

00:42:21.460 --> 00:42:23.970
So what we were
seeing was that it

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:25.680
was quite challenging
for companies

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:31.920
to just take a quick look,
try and experience a work

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:35.310
profile and this sort of
application deployment

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:36.492
and so on on a device.

00:42:36.492 --> 00:42:38.700
So we created something
called the Android Management

00:42:38.700 --> 00:42:39.510
Experience.

00:42:39.510 --> 00:42:42.660
If you search for this in
Google Play on a device,

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:48.290
you'll be able to download
it, with a couple of clicks,

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:51.480
set up a demo
company environment,

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:55.020
and go through and practice
with deploying applications

00:42:55.020 --> 00:42:56.910
and policy and so on.

00:42:56.910 --> 00:42:59.910
So a super quick way, in
just a couple of minutes,

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:02.020
to get up and running
and an experience

00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:04.129
it and show colleagues
or whatever.

00:43:04.129 --> 00:43:05.670
And then you can
kind of step through

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:10.350
into a fully managed
wider deployment.

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:12.960
Enterprise.google.com/android
has a whole bunch

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:15.710
of information around
[INAUDIBLE] directory,

